    analysis: `<p><strong>Processing.</strong> For each field, count every cell whose centre lies inside the field of view, in five categories (interphase, prophase, metaphase, anaphase, telophase). Mitotic index of a field = (prophase + metaphase + anaphase + telophase) ÷ total cells. For a slide, add the counts of all the fields first and then divide (a pooled index): this uses every cell equally. A mean of the field values is close to it when the fields hold similar numbers of cells.</p>
<p><strong>Worked example (simulated data).</strong> Six fields of one meristem squash:</p>${fieldTbl("control", 6)}
<p>Field 1: (3 + 2 + 0 + 0) ÷ 50 = 5 ÷ 50 = <strong>0.10</strong>. All six fields together: dividing cells = ${CS[1]} + ${CS[2]} + ${CS[3]} + ${CS[4]} = ${CD}; total = ${CT}; pooled mitotic index = ${CD} ÷ ${CT} = <strong>${f3(CD / CT)}</strong> (${f1((CD / CT) * 100)} %). The single fields range from ${f2(2 / 50)} (field 4) to ${f3(10 / 48)} (field 6), which is why several fields are needed.</p>
<p><strong>Estimating time from proportions.</strong> If, hypothetically, the cell cycle in this meristem takes 20 hours, and the proportion of cells in a stage equals the proportion of the cycle spent in it, time in mitosis ≈ ${f3(CD / CT)} × 20 h = ${f2((CD / CT) * 20)} h (about ${Math.round((CD / CT) * 20 * 60)} minutes) and time in prophase ≈ (${CS[1]} ÷ ${CT}) × 20 h = ${f2((CS[1] / CT) * 20)} h. Only ${CS[3]} anaphase cell was seen, giving (1 ÷ 300) × 20 h ≈ ${Math.round((1 / 300) * 20 * 60)} minutes: a value based on one cell is very unreliable, because a short stage contributes very few cells to any sample.</p>
<p><strong>Comparing samples.</strong> Compare pooled values from samples counted the same way, and compare the difference with the spread between fields of the same slide. In the simulated data, the zone of elongation gave ${div(ES)} dividing cell out of ${tot(ES)} (${f3(div(ES) / tot(ES))}), far below the meristem, because cells there have stopped dividing and are growing longer.</p>
<p><strong>Interpreting a change.</strong> The mitotic index is the <strong>proportion</strong> of cells in mitosis at the moment the cells were killed, not the rate of division. A higher index can mean that more cells are entering mitosis, or that cells are taking longer to get through it (for example if spindle fibres cannot form, cells are held in mitosis and never reach anaphase). A lower index can mean that fewer cells are reaching mitosis (for example if DNA cannot be replicated in S phase). Look at the stages as well: an index that has risen with no anaphase or telophase cells points to a block in mitosis. Never assume that a substance raises or lowers the index: it depends on where in the cycle it acts and for how long.</p>
<p><strong>Evaluating.</strong> Identification is a judgement: early prophase can look like interphase, and cells caught between stages are hard to classify. Agree criteria before counting, use the fine focus, record uncertain cells as such, and have a second person count the same fields. Count enough cells (several hundred) in fields chosen at random or systematically.</p>
<p><strong>Is a difference significant?</strong> <span class="badge amber">stretch</span> The specification's maths skills include the chi-squared test, and the handbook suggests testing the difference in the number of dividing cells at two distances from the root tip. Put the numbers of dividing and non-dividing cells for the two samples in a 2 × 2 contingency table and use a chi-squared test (1 degree of freedom) to decide whether the difference is likely to be due to chance.</p>`,
